<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdowsi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">940–<a href="/years/1025">1025</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abul-Qāsem Mansur bin Hasan Tosi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdowsi, the pen name of Abul-Qāsem Mansur bin Hasan Tosi, was a Persian poet and the author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> (&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Book of Kings</a>&quot;), one of the longest epic poems created by a single poet. He was born in 940 into a family of dehqans, Persian landowners, in the village of Paj near Tus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, then under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>. Drawing on the Abu-Mansuri Shahnameh, he finished a first version in the 990s; when the Ghaznavids overthrew the Samanids, he rewrote sections to praise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mahmud and completed the epic in 1010. How well Mahmud rewarded him is disputed and became the subject of legends. The poem joins the culture of pre-Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to that of Islamic Iran.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdowsi.jpg" alt="Ferdowsi" width="330" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ca. 1476–86 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imaginary_depiction_of_the_poet_Firdausi._Folio_from_a_Khavarannama_(The_Book_of_the_East)_of_ibn_Husam_al-Din,_1476-1486_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ferdowsi</b> completed the Shahnameh, the national epic of Iran, around 1010 and presented it to Mahmud, although the story that the sultan rewarded him poorly is a later legend that cannot be verified.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Persian culture carries Zoroastrian traces in its calendar, its festivals, above all Nowruz, and the epic Shahnameh of <b>Ferdowsi</b>, completed around 1010, which retells the legends of ancient Iran.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ferdowsi</b>, in the Shahnameh, used relatively few Arabic words, and in the 20th century an academy in Iran coined Persian words to replace foreign ones.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the story in <b>Ferdowsi</b>'s Shah Nama, Borzuy had gone to India in search of a herb that revives the dead and was told that the herb is the scientist and the book the way to knowledge.</blockquote><a href="/work/panchatantra">Panchatantra · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>For the most part it passed into a Persian prose work of the same name, compiled in <b>Ferdowsi</b>'s earlier life in his native Tus; many such prose accounts existed, among them the Shahnameh of Abu-Mansur.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After some thirty years' work, the poet <b>Ferdowsi</b> finished his epic of the kings of Iran, around 50,000 couplets long.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-8">8 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
